      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given the temperature function \\( T(t) = -t^2 + 12t + 50 \\), we need to find the value of \\( t \\) when the temperature is exactly 77 degrees. This means we need to solve the equation:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 = 77\n\\]\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation to standard quadratic form:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t + 50 - 77 = 0\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n-t^2 + 12t - 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNext, we multiply the entire equation by -1 to make the coefficient of \\( t^2 \\) positive:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 12t + 27 = 0\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -12 \\), and \\( c = 27 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{-(-12) \\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 27}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying inside the square root:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{144 - 108}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 + 6}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{12 - 6}{2} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3\n\\]\n\nThe largest \\( t \\) value at which the temperature was exactly 77 degrees is \\( t = 9 \\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
